DESCRIPTION:Police Violence on a Group of Satyagrahis\, Gifted by Roopa &amp\; G.V. Ravi Shankar\nThis photograph from India\, 1930 shows a group of satyagrahis interlocking their arms\, as the force of a colonial police officer attempts to break apart this protest. We do not know where this photograph was specifically taken or what protest they were a part of\; and yet the strength of a collective gathering is evident. Several images such as this reflect the mass nature of the Indian independence movement. People from different walks of life\, even with differing ideologies and social positions\, united for the common cause of independence. \n\nImagine the moments before and after this photograph was taken. What brought these people together? How did they hear about this protest? Did they anticipate the violent repression that they eventually faced? What happened to them after this moment\, were they injured or arrested? Did they continue to protest despite the violence\, or was the call to gather amplified by it? Are these photographs just a memory of the past or do they hold a mirror to a reality of the present? Is this photograph bound by time or does it continue to inform and inspire?\n\nPhotographs such as these and other archival documents implore us to look beyond the iconic and popular imagery of the independence movement. They also give us an opportunity to engage with the idea of gatherings\, their particular contexts in Indian history and beyond.\n\nIn this two-day workshop\, Acts of Gathering\, visual artist Uzma Mohsin will take participants through photographs\, documents\, and other visual material from the Gandhi Collection. Participants will engage with Mohsin’s own work on photographs\, archives\, and public histories. They will think through the idea of political gatherings and develop a response to the material in the collection.
